Auer. That reflects who operated the keyboard, not who made the work: the
application was specified, designed and decided jointly by both holders, and
the design directions the code follows ("2a", "3a", "4c" in the comments) came
out of that collaboration. Both are therefore named here, and both must consent
to any licence change.

If either holder disagrees with this reading, this file is the place to correct
it — before anything is published, not after.

What was ported, and from where
-------------------------------

Source: `cauer71/AndroidDev`, path `apps/cie-reader`, at commit 7ab0d20
("Ship 1.8 to the closed test"), version 1.8 / versionCode 11, read on
2026-08-20.

Carried over substantially unchanged, and therefore derived work:

  * the data model (`DocumentData`, `Authenticity`, `DocumentType`,
    `RecordProvenance`, `AccessKey`, `StoredDocument`);
  * the three parsers (`LicenceScan`, `MrzScan`, `CanScan`) including the
    reasoning in their comments, which is the actual value in them;
  * the archive file format — same JSON keys, same version number 8, same
    envelope of IV length, IV, ciphertext;
  * the export text and its JSON field names, which a downstream report
    importer depends on;
  * all user-facing wording in English, German and Italian;
  * the six colour schemes (card / passport / licence, light and dark);
  * the measured OCR test corpus and the two tests built on it.

Written for this platform, and not derived:

  * everything under `App/` — SwiftUI in place of Compose;
  * the CoreNFC reader session in place of Android reader mode;
  * Vision text recognition in place of ML Kit;
  * CryptoKit and Keychain in place of the Android Keystore.

